            "chapter_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning",
            "content_sha256": "29bc844a39c962c17d77aa26ef6c3ddd10f0902dea8441940aa2c3a29aeb5eb6",
            "context_id": "ctx_be8318b49ee8de45eb672658",
            "evidence_roles": [
              "BACKUP"
            ],
            "matched_surface": "discrete random variables",
            "positive_evidence_eligible": true,
            "sentence_id": "sentence_911ce5257dff458d08c8facd",
            "source_text": "They have some technical difficulties that make them more challenging to work with compared to discrete random variables.",
            "synthetic": false
          },
          {
            "block_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning_random_variables_b002",
            "boundary_only": false,
            "chapter_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning",
            "content_sha256": "0caaecc41804066e986cdabe4b882821d424d485a00317d148479bef51e44139",
            "context_id": "ctx_dcc2558c83757b30c8a58c12",
            "evidence_roles": [
              "POSITIVE_DEFINITION_PROPOSAL",
              "POSITIVE_POS_PROPOSAL",
              "PRIMARY"
            ],
            "matched_surface": "discrete random variables",
            "positive_evidence_eligible": true,
            "sentence_id": "sentence_66e5c5fb8607b702d5e007b8",
            "source_text": "In :numref:`sec_prob` we saw the basics of how to work with discrete random variables, which in our case refer to those random variables which take either a finite set of possible values, or the integers.",
            "synthetic": false
          },
          {
            "block_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning_random_variables_b055",
            "boundary_only": false,
            "chapter_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning",
            "content_sha256": "fd7a10f661c846dd78ef609e60f62800629b085a2d964699b9163347c3406a42",
            "context_id": "ctx_ddc48912aae8d4e18d86b760",
            "evidence_roles": [
              "BACKUP",
              "POSITIVE_DEFINITION_PROPOSAL"
            ],
            "matched_surface": "discrete random variables",
            "positive_evidence_eligible": true,
            "sentence_id": "sentence_db31d642ac437bba15285c17",
            "source_text": "In this example, we see one of the benefits of working with the c.d.f., the ability to deal with continuous or discrete random variables in the same framework, or indeed mixtures of the two (flip a coin: if heads return the roll of a die, if tails return the distance of a dart throw from the center of a dart board).",
            "synthetic": false
          },
          {
            "block_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning_information_theory_b057",
            "boundary_only": false,
            "chapter_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning",
            "content_sha256": "77cf34bebf11b04605ca49d9f4f96b7785e3db300a1665afdedc503df14bfbd2",
            "context_id": "ctx_e7f67dd37287b32e45850474",
            "evidence_roles": [
              "POSITIVE_POS_PROPOSAL",
              "PRIMARY"
            ],
            "matched_surface": "discrete random variables",
            "positive_evidence_eligible": true,
            "sentence_id": "sentence_91ab43549975f0911615e1e7",
            "source_text": "Precisely, on the one hand, if $(X, Y)$ is a pair of discrete random variables, then",
            "synthetic": false
          },
          {
            "block_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning_maximum_likelihood_b054",
            "boundary_only": false,
            "chapter_id": "d2l_appendix_mathematics_for_deep_learning",
